\label{ch:exception-handling}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:exception-intro}

Exception handling is a language mechanism for dealing with exceptional conditions---errors, unexpected inputs, or unusual control flow---that disrupt the normal execution path. Rather than requiring every function to check return codes and propagate errors manually, exceptions provide a structured way to transfer control from the point where an error is detected to the point where it is handled, potentially across many intervening function calls.

The implementation of exception handling is one of the most intricate tasks facing a compiler. It must support non-local control transfer, maintain the integrity of the runtime state during unwinding, and do so with minimal overhead in the common case where no exception is thrown. This chapter examines the models, mechanisms, and implementation strategies for exception handling across modern programming languages.

\section{Exception Models: Termination vs.\ Resumption}\label{sec:exception-models}

\subsection{Termination Semantics}\label{sec:termination}

\begin{definition}[Termination Model]\label{def:termination}
In the \emph{termination model} of exception handling, when an exception is raised, the stack is unwound to the nearest enclosing handler. The code that raised the exception does not resume; the exception \emph{terminates} the current computation and transfers control to the handler.
\end{definition}

Most modern languages use termination semantics: C++, Java, C\#, Python, Rust, and Go all use this model. Once an exception is caught, execution continues after the handler; the code that raised the exception is not resumed.

\subsection{Resumption Semantics}\label{sec:resumption}

\begin{definition}[Resumption Model]\label{def:resumption}
In the \emph{resumption model} of exception handling, the handler may \emph{resume} the computation at the point where the exception was raised, after taking corrective action.
\end{definition}

Resumption semantics were used in early languages like CLU and are found in some specialized systems. They are more expressive but also more complex to implement and reason about.

\subsection{Comparison}\label{sec:model-comparison}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Termination vs.\ resumption exception models}
\label{tab:exception-models}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Termination} & \textbf{Resumption} \\
\hline
Stack state & Unwound to handler & Preserved \\
Resumption & Not possible & Handler can resume \\
Complexity & Lower & Higher \\
Reasoning & Simpler & Harder (re-entrant) \\
Languages & C++, Java, Python, Rust & CLU, Beta \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Zero-Cost Exceptions (Table-Based)}\label{sec:zero-cost}

\subsection{The Zero-Cost Principle}\label{sec:zero-cost-principle}

\begin{definition}[Zero-Cost Exceptions]\label{def:zero-cost}
\emph{Zero-cost exceptions} are an implementation strategy where the normal (non-exceptional) execution path incurs no overhead from the exception handling mechanism. The cost of exception handling is paid only when an exception is actually thrown.
\end{definition}

The key insight is that the compiler knows, at compile time, exactly which instructions are covered by which exception handlers. This information can be encoded in static tables that are consulted only during exception handling.

\subsection{Table-Based Implementation}\label{sec:table-based}

In the table-based approach (used by Itanium C++ ABI, Rust, and others):

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The compiler generates \emph{exception tables} (also called \emph{landing pad tables} or \emph{ unwind tables}) that describe, for each instruction range, which handlers are active.
  \item The normal code path contains no exception handling overhead---no checks, no setup, no teardown.
  \item When an exception is thrown, the runtime walks the stack, consulting the tables at each frame to determine how to unwind and where to transfer control.
\end{enumerate}

