\subsection{Landing Pads}\label{sec:landing-pads}

A \emph{landing pad} is a special code sequence that the compiler generates to handle the arrival of an exception at a particular function frame.

\begin{definition}[Landing Pad]\label{def:landing-pad}
A \emph{landing pad} is a code block within a function that is the target of exception-driven control transfer. When the unwinder determines that a handler exists in the current frame, it adjusts the stack and instruction pointer to the landing pad, which then:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Receives the exception object (typically in designated registers).
  \item Performs any necessary cleanup (calling destructors, releasing resources).
  \item Either handles the exception (transferring control to the catch block) or resumes unwinding.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\section{Stack Unwinding}\label{sec:stack-unwinding}

\subsection{The Unwinding Process}\label{sec:unwinding-process}

When an exception is thrown, the runtime must unwind the stack to find a handler:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Search phase}: walk the stack frames from the current frame upward, consulting the exception tables to find a matching handler.
  \item \textbf{Cleanup phase}: walk the stack again, executing cleanup code (destructors, \texttt{finally} blocks) in each frame between the throw point and the handler.
  \item \textbf{Transfer phase}: transfer control to the handler.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Personality Routines}\label{sec:personality-routines}

Each function frame has an associated \emph{personality routine} that interprets the exception tables for that frame:

\begin{definition}[Personality Routine]\label{def:personality}
A \emph{personality routine} is a function called by the unwinder for each stack frame during exception handling. It determines:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Whether the current frame has a handler for the thrown exception.
  \item What cleanup actions must be performed.
  \item How to adjust the frame for the landing pad.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Different languages may use different personality routines. For example:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{\_\_gxx\_personality\_v0}: C++ personality, handles type-based matching.
  \item \texttt{rust\_personality\_v0}: Rust personality, handles Rust's panic mechanism.
  \item \texttt{\_\_gcc\_rust\_personality}: a newer Rust personality using the GCC infrastructure.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Frame Unwinding}\label{sec:frame-unwinding}

To unwind a frame, the runtime must:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Determine the return address (the instruction that called the current function).
  \item Restore the stack pointer to its value before the function's prologue.
  \item Restore callee-saved registers.
  \item Transfer control to the appropriate landing pad or continue unwinding.
\end{enumerate}

This information is encoded in the \texttt{.eh\_frame} section (ELF) or \texttt{.pdata}/\texttt{.xdata} (PE/Windows).

\section{Exception Handling in Different Languages}\label{sec:exception-languages}

\subsection{C++ Exceptions}\label{sec:cpp-exceptions}

C++ uses zero-cost exceptions with type-based matching:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Exceptions are objects (of any type, but typically derived from \texttt{std::exception}).
  \item Catch clauses match by type, with derived-to-base conversion.
  \item Stack unwinding calls destructors for all automatic objects in unwound frames (\emph{stack unwinding}).
  \item \texttt{noexcept} indicates that a function does not throw; violating this calls \texttt{std::terminate}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Java Exceptions}\label{sec:java-exceptions}

Java uses zero-cost exceptions with checked and unchecked exception types:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Checked exceptions}: must be declared in the method signature or caught. Enforced at compile time.
  \item \textbf{Unchecked exceptions}: \texttt{RuntimeException} and its subclasses; need not be declared.
  \item \textbf{Finally blocks}: executed whether or not an exception is thrown.
  \item \textbf{Try-with-resources}: automatic resource management (auto-closeable).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Rust Panics}\label{sec:rust-panics}

Rust uses a panic mechanism for unrecoverable errors:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{panic!} aborts the current thread after unwinding.
  \item \texttt{catch\_unwind} can intercept panics (but is discouraged).
  \item \texttt{Drop} implementations run during unwinding.
  \item \texttt{panic = "abort"} disables unwinding entirely (smaller code, no exception tables).
  \item Recoverable errors use \texttt{Result<T, E>} rather than exceptions.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Go: panic/recover}\label{sec:go-exceptions}

Go's exception mechanism is deliberately minimal:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{panic} initiates panicking, running deferred functions.
  \item \texttt{recover} (called in a deferred function) stops panicking and returns the panic value.
  \item Normal errors use explicit error returns (\texttt{(result, error)}).
  \item The runtime uses a linked list of \texttt{\_defer} structs on each goroutine's stack.
\end{itemize}

\section{Implementation: Landing Pads and Personality Routines}\label{sec:landing-pad-impl}

\subsection{Landing Pad Code Generation}\label{sec:lp-codegen}

The compiler generates landing pads as special basic blocks in the function's control-flow graph:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Each \texttt{try} region is associated with one or more landing pads.
  \item The landing pad receives the exception object and a selector indicating the exception type.
  \item The landing pad contains cleanup code and a switch on the selector to dispatch to the appropriate catch clause.
  \item If no catch matches, the landing pad resumes unwinding (calls \texttt{\_Unwind\_Resume}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Exception Tables Format}\label{sec:eh-tables}

The \texttt{.gcc\_except\_table} section encodes the mapping from instruction addresses to landing pads:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Call-site table}: for each call instruction (or range of instructions), records the landing pad and the action table index.
  \item \textbf{Action table}: a chain of (type filter, next action) pairs. A type filter of 0 means ``cleanup only''; a positive value indexes into the type table.
  \item \textbf{Type table}: references to type info structures for type-based matching.
\end{itemize}

\section{Performance Considerations}\label{sec:exception-performance}

\subsection{Zero-Cost Overhead Analysis}\label{sec:zero-cost-overhead}

Zero-cost exceptions have the following overhead profile:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Overhead of zero-cost exceptions}
\label{tab:exception-overhead}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Phase} & \textbf{Normal Path} & \textbf{Exception Path} \\
\hline
Setup/teardown & None & N/A \\
Code size & +10--30\% (tables, landing pads) & N/A \\
Throw cost & N/A & Very high (stack walk, cleanup) \\
Catch cost & N/A & Moderate (type matching) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The trade-off is clear: zero-cost exceptions optimize for the common case (no exception thrown) at the expense of the exceptional case. This is appropriate when exceptions are truly exceptional---rare, error-path events.

\subsection{Setjmp/Longjmp Model}\label{sec:setjmp-model}

An alternative to zero-cost exceptions is the \emph{setjmp/longjmp} model (used by older C++ implementations and some embedded systems):

\begin{itemize}
  \item On entering a \texttt{try} block, call \texttt{setjmp} to save the current context.
  \item On throwing, call \texttt{longjmp} to restore the saved context.
  \item The saved context is stored in a linked list on the stack.
\end{itemize}

This model has overhead on every \texttt{try} entry/exit but cheaper throws. It is appropriate when throws are frequent or when code size is critical.

\subsection{When Exceptions Are Not Exceptional}\label{sec:not-exceptional}

If exceptions are used for common control flow (e.g., iterator exhaustion, optional values), the zero-cost model becomes a liability. In such cases:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Use explicit error types (\texttt{Result}, \texttt{Option}) instead.
  \item Use the setjmp/longjmp model if exceptions must be used.
  \item Consider language-level alternatives (coroutines, pattern matching).
\end{itemize}

\section{Exception Handling in the IR}\label{sec:exception-ir}

\subsection{Representing Exceptions in the IR}\label{sec:exception-ir-rep}

The compiler's IR must represent exception handling constructs:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Invoke instruction}: a call that can transfer control to a landing pad on exception.
  \item \textbf{Landing pad}: a basic block that handles incoming exceptions.
  \item \textbf{Resume instruction}: re-raises the current exception after cleanup.
  \item \textbf{Catch switch}: dispatches to the appropriate catch handler based on exception type.
  \item \textbf{Cleanup pad}: executes cleanup code (destructors, \texttt{finally} blocks).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Exception Handling in LLVM IR}\label{sec:llvm-eh}

LLVM's IR represents exception handling with several instructions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{invoke}: like \texttt{call}, but with a normal destination and an unwind destination (landing pad).
  \item \texttt{landingpad}: the entry point for exception handling in a function. Returns an exception value and a selector.
  \item \texttt{resume}: re-raises the current exception.
  \item \texttt{catchswitch}, \texttt{catchret}, \texttt{catchpad}: Windows-style exception handling.
  \item \texttt{cleanuppad}, \texttt{cleanupret}: cleanup-only exception handling.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Lowering Exceptions to Machine Code}\label{sec:exception-lowering}

The lowering from IR to machine code involves:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Converting \texttt{invoke} to \texttt{call} + branch on exception.
  \item Generating landing pad code that receives the exception and dispatches.
  \item Emitting exception tables (\texttt{.gcc\_except\_table}) that describe the mapping from code ranges to landing pads.
  \item Emitting unwind information (\texttt{.eh\_frame}) for stack traversal.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Alternatives to Exceptions}\label{sec:exception-alternatives}

\subsection{Result Types}\label{sec:result-types}

Many modern languages use explicit result types instead of exceptions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Rust}: \texttt{Result<T, E>} for recoverable errors, \texttt{panic!} for unrecoverable errors.
  \item \textbf{Go}: explicit error returns \texttt{(T, error)}.
  \item \textbf{Swift}: \texttt{throws} functions with \texttt{do/catch} (similar to exceptions but with compile-time checking).
  \item \textbf{Haskell}: \texttt{Either e a} monad for error handling.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Effect Handlers}\label{sec:effect-handlers}

Effect handlers provide a general mechanism for handling exceptional behavior:

\begin{definition}[Effect Handler]\label{def:effect-handler}
An \emph{effect handler} is a mechanism for intercepting and handling computational effects (including exceptions, state, I/O) in a composable way. The handler can resume the computation, provide an alternative result, or propagate the effect.
\end{definition}

Effect handlers generalize exceptions by allowing:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Multiple handlers for the same effect.
  \item Resumption (like resumption exceptions).
  \item Composable effect handling (handlers can be stacked).
\end{itemize}

