\section{Zero-Cost Exception Implementation Details}\label{sec:zero-cost-details}

The zero-cost exception model is the dominant implementation strategy in modern systems languages. This section provides a detailed examination of how it works at the machine level.

\subsection{The Exception Table Format}\label{sec:eh-table-format}

The \texttt{.gcc\_except\_table} section encodes the mapping from code ranges to exception handlers using a compact binary format:

\begin{verbatim}
  .gcc_except_table layout:
  +---------------------------+
  | Header (LP start, TT base)|
  +---------------------------+
  | Call-site table header    |
  |   (format, length)        |
  +---------------------------+
  | Call-site entry 0:        |
  |   (start, len, landing    |
  |    pad, action index)     |
  +---------------------------+
  | Call-site entry 1:        |
  |   ...                     |
  +---------------------------+
  | Action table:             |
  |   (type filter, next      |
  |    action offset)         |
  +---------------------------+
  | Type table:               |
  |   (typeinfo pointers)     |
  +---------------------------+
\end{verbatim}

\begin{definition}[Call-Site Entry]\label{def:call-site}
Each call-site entry $(\mathrm{start}, \mathrm{length}, \mathrm{lp}, \mathrm{action})$ specifies:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathrm{start}$: the start address of the code range (relative to the function's start).
  \item $\mathrm{length}$: the length of the code range.
  \item $\mathrm{lp}$: the landing pad address (relative to the function's start), or 0 if no handler.
  \item $\mathrm{action}$: an index into the action table (1-based), or 0 for cleanup-only, or $-1$ for no action.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Unwinding Protocol}\label{sec:unwinding-protocol}

The Itanium C++ ABI defines a two-phase unwinding protocol:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Two-Phase Exception Unwinding}\label{alg:two-phase}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\State \textbf{Phase 1: Search}
\State $\mathrm{context} \gets \mathrm{current\_frame}$
\While{$\mathrm{context} \neq \mathrm{null}$}
  \State $\mathrm{personality} \gets \mathrm{get\_personality}(\mathrm{context})$
  \State $\mathrm{result} \gets \mathrm{personality}(\mathrm{SEARCH}, \mathrm{exception}, \mathrm{context})$
  \If{$\mathrm{result} = \mathrm{HANDLER\_FOUND}$}
    \State \textbf{break} \Comment{Handler found; proceed to Phase 2}
  \ElsIf{$\mathrm{result} = \mathrm{CONTINUE\_SEARCH}$}
    \State $\mathrm{context} \gets \mathrm{next\_frame}(\mathrm{context})$
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\If{$\mathrm{context} = \mathrm{null}$}
  \State \textbf{abort} \Comment{No handler found}
\EndIf
\State
\State \textbf{Phase 2: Cleanup and Transfer}
\State $\mathrm{context} \gets \mathrm{current\_frame}$
\While{$\mathrm{context} \neq \mathrm{handler\_frame}$}
  \State $\mathrm{personality} \gets \mathrm{get\_personality}(\mathrm{context})$
  \State $\mathrm{personality}(\mathrm{CLEANUP}, \mathrm{exception}, \mathrm{context})$ \Comment{Run destructors}
  \State $\mathrm{context} \gets \mathrm{next\_frame}(\mathrm{context})$
\EndWhile
\State $\mathrm{Transfer\_to\_handler}(\mathrm{handler\_frame}, \mathrm{exception})$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Personality Routine Implementation}\label{sec:personality-impl}

The personality routine is the heart of the exception handling mechanism. It is called by the unwinder for each frame during both the search and cleanup phases:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Personality Routine (Simplified)}\label{alg:personality}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{PersonalityRoutine}{$\mathrm{version}, \mathrm{actions}, \mathrm{exception\_class}, \mathrm{exception}, \mathrm{context}$}
  \State $\mathrm{ip} \gets \mathrm{get\_IP}(\mathrm{context})$
  \State $\mathrm{func\_start} \gets \mathrm{get\_func\_start}(\mathrm{context})$
  \State $\mathrm{except\_table} \gets \mathrm{get\_except\_table}(\mathrm{context})$
  \State
  \If{$\mathrm{actions} = \mathrm{SEARCH}$}
    \State $\mathrm{entry} \gets \mathrm{find\_call\_site}(\mathrm{except\_table}, \mathrm{ip} - \mathrm{func\_start})$
    \If{$\mathrm{entry}.\mathrm{lp} = 0$}
      \State \Return $\mathrm{CONTINUE\_SEARCH}$
    \EndIf
    \State $\mathrm{action} \gets \mathrm{get\_action}(\mathrm{except\_table}, \mathrm{entry}.\mathrm{action})$
    \While{$\mathrm{action} \neq \mathrm{null}$}
      \If{$\mathrm{action}.\mathrm{type\_filter} > 0$}
        \State $\mathrm{type} \gets \mathrm{type\_table}[\mathrm{action}.\mathrm{type\_filter}]$
        \If{$\mathrm{matches}(\mathrm{exception}, \mathrm{type})$}
          \State \Return $\mathrm{HANDLER\_FOUND}$
        \EndIf
      \ElsIf{$\mathrm{action}.\mathrm{type\_filter} = 0$}
        \State \Return $\mathrm{HANDLER\_FOUND}$ \Comment{Cleanup handler}
      \EndIf
      \State $\mathrm{action} \gets \mathrm{action}.\mathrm{next}$
    \EndWhile
    \State \Return $\mathrm{CONTINUE\_SEARCH}$
  \ElsIf{$\mathrm{actions} = \mathrm{CLEANUP}$}
    \State $\mathrm{entry} \gets \mathrm{find\_call\_site}(\mathrm{except\_table}, \mathrm{ip} - \mathrm{func\_start})$
    \State $\mathrm{set\_IP}(\mathrm{context}, \mathrm{func\_start} + \mathrm{entry}.\mathrm{lp})$
    \State \Return $\mathrm{INSTALL\_CONTEXT}$
  \EndIf
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Landing Pad Code Structure}\label{sec:lp-structure}

A landing pad is a special basic block that receives control from the unwinder. Its structure is:

\begin{verbatim}
  landing_pad:
    ; Receive exception object and selector from unwinder
    ; (typically in registers: %rdi = exception, %esi = selector)
    %exc_ptr   = call i8* @llvm.eh.exception()
    %selector  = call i32 @llvm.eh.selector()
    
    ; Check if this is a cleanup or a catch
    %is_catch  = icmp sgt i32 %selector, 0
    
    ; If cleanup, run destructors then resume
    br i1 %is_catch, label %check_type, label %cleanup
    
  check_type:
    ; Switch on selector to dispatch to the right catch clause
    switch i32 %selector, label %no_match [
      i32 1, label %catch_int      ; catch (int)
      i32 2, label %catch_runtime  ; catch (std::runtime_error)
      i32 3, label %catch_all      ; catch (...)
    ]
    
  catch_int:
    ; Handle integer exception
    ...
    br label %end_try
    
  cleanup:
    ; Run destructors for automatic variables
    call void @_ZN3FooD1Ev(%struct.Foo* %foo)  ; ~Foo()
    ; Resume unwinding
    call void @_Unwind_Resume(i8* %exc_ptr)
    unreachable
    
  end_try:
    ; Continue execution after the try block
    ...
\end{verbatim}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Cleaning Up a Partially Constructed Array}

A landing pad that always destroys every array element assumes that all constructors have returned successfully. If construction throws halfway through a loop, that action would call destructors for objects that do not exist. A single cleanup pad can handle every failing iteration by recording the length of the successfully initialized prefix.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Construct $n$ elements in increasing order in storage owned by this routine. A failed element constructor is responsible for its own successfully initialized subobjects; the outer pad may destroy only complete elements. Destructors and release of the raw storage do not throw. Use an invoke-like instruction with separate normal and exceptional successors, so that only the normal successor advances the prefix count.

\begin{sudocode}[Track the constructed prefix]
\Require Raw array storage $A$, length $n$
\Ensure Full array or storage released and exception propagated
\State $k\gets 0$
\While{$k<n$}
  \State Invoke constructor for $A[k]$
  \State On exception $e$, jump to cleanup with $(e,k)$
  \State $k\gets k+1$ \Comment{Only on the normal edge}
\EndWhile
\State \Return ownership of $A$
\State \textbf{cleanup} $(e,k)$:
\While{$k>0$}
  \State $k\gets k-1$ \Comment{Destroy in reverse construction order}
  \State Destroy $A[k]$
\EndWhile
\State Release raw storage for $A$
\State Resume unwinding with $e$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} For $n=5$, constructors at indices 0 and 1 succeed, but index 2 throws. The landing pad receives $k=2$. It destroys indices 1 and 0, releases the allocation once, and resumes the original exception. It never calls the complete-element destructor for index 2, 3, or 4. If index 0 throws, only the storage release runs.

\Cref{fig:visual-89} marks the prefix passed to the landing pad at
the point of the throw. A constructor call does not extend the
prefix until it returns normally.

