\textbf{Invariant.} At each invoke, precisely the prefix $A[0:k)$ consists of complete elements owned by this scope. Cleanup costs $O(k)$ destructor calls, with no handler duplication across iterations. On normal completion ownership transfers to the caller, so the pad is not executed. If a destructor can throw during unwinding, language-specific rules govern the second exception; the simple resume path is no longer sufficient.

\subsection{Exception Object Allocation}\label{sec:exception-alloc}

The exception object must survive the stack unwinding, so it cannot be allocated on the stack:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Heap allocation}: the \texttt{throw} expression allocates the exception object on the heap (using \texttt{\_\_cxa\_allocate\_exception}).
  \item \textbf{Exception header}: a header is prepended to the exception object, containing the exception class, destructor pointer, and reference count.
  \item \textbf{Reference counting}: the exception object is reference-counted; it is deallocated when the last catch clause releases it.
  \item \textbf{Rethrow}: when an exception is rethrown (\texttt{throw;} in C++), the same object is propagated without copying.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  block/.style={draw, minimum width=4cm, minimum height=0.5cm, font=\small, align=center},
]
  \node[block, fill=TomeExBg] (header) at (0, 2) {Exception Header\\(class, destructor, refcount)};
  \node[block, fill=TomeBg] (object) at (0, 1) {Exception Object\\(user data)};
