%% Section: Exception Handling Across Language Boundaries
%% ============================================================
\section{Exception Handling Across Language Boundaries}\label{sec:cross-language-eh}

When programs consist of code compiled by different compilers or in different languages, exception handling must work correctly across the boundaries.

\subsection{The Foreign Exception Problem}\label{sec:foreign-exceptions}

When an exception passes through a frame compiled by a different compiler (or in a different language), the personality routine must handle it correctly:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{C++ through C}: C frames have no personality routine; the unwinder skips them. But C frames may hold resources (file handles, locks) that need cleanup.
  \item \textbf{C++ through Rust}: Rust's personality routine (\texttt{rust\_personality\_v0}) handles Rust panics but must also forward foreign exceptions.
  \item \textbf{Java through JNI}: Java exceptions passing through native code require explicit handling.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Exception Class and Identification}\label{sec:exception-class}

The Itanium ABI uses an \emph{exception class} (an 8-byte identifier) to distinguish exceptions from different languages:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Exception class identifiers}
\label{tab:exception-class}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Language} & \textbf{Exception Class} & \textbf{Encoding} \\
\hline
C++ & \texttt{GNUCC++\textbackslash 0} & \texttt{0x474E5543432B2B00} \\
Java & \texttt{GNUJAVA\textbackslash 0} & \texttt{0x474E554A41564100} \\
Rust & \texttt{MOZRS\textbackslash 0\textbackslash 0\textbackslash 0} & (implementation-defined) \\
D & \texttt{DNDD\textbackslash 0\textbackslash 0\textbackslash 0\textbackslash 0} & (implementation-defined) \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The personality routine checks the exception class to determine whether it can handle the exception. If it cannot, it returns \texttt{CONTINUE\_SEARCH}, allowing the unwinder to continue to the next frame.

\subsection{Cross-Language Exception Handling in Practice}\label{sec:cross-lang-practice}

Modern systems handle cross-language exceptions as follows:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{C++/Rust interop}: Rust's \texttt{extern "C"} functions catch panics at the boundary and convert them to C error codes (since C has no exception mechanism).
  \item \textbf{Java/native interop}: JNI functions catch Java exceptions and store them in the JVM thread state; native code checks for pending exceptions.
  \item \textbf{WebAssembly}: Wasm uses a \texttt{try/catch} proposal that maps to the host's exception mechanism.
\end{itemize}

