%% Section: Performance Engineering for Exceptions
%% ============================================================
\section{Performance Engineering for Exception Handling}\label{sec:exception-perf}

\subsection{Measuring Exception Overhead}\label{sec:measuring-overhead}

The overhead of exception handling can be measured at several levels:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Exception overhead measurements (representative)}
\label{tab:exception-measurements}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Zero-cost} & \textbf{Setjmp/longjmp} & \textbf{Error codes} \\
\hline
Normal path overhead & 0\% & 5--15\% & 1--3\% \\
Code size increase & 10--30\% & 2--5\% & 5--10\% \\
Throw cost (stack depth 10) & 1--5 $\mu$s & 0.1--0.5 $\mu$s & N/A \\
Throw cost (stack depth 100) & 10--50 $\mu$s & 0.1--0.5 $\mu$s & N/A \\
Throw cost (stack depth 1000) & 100--500 $\mu$s & 0.1--0.5 $\mu$s & N/A \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The zero-cost model's throw cost scales with the stack depth because the unwinder must walk every frame. This makes exceptions unsuitable for high-frequency error paths.

\subsection{Optimizing Exception-Heavy Code}\label{sec:optimizing-eh-code}

When exceptions are used more frequently than intended, several optimizations can help:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Exception coalescing}: combine multiple throw sites that share a handler into a single throw.
  \item \textbf{Inlining landing pads}: inline the landing pad code at the call site to avoid the overhead of an indirect jump.
  \item \textbf{Cold code layout}: place landing pads and exception tables in cold sections to improve instruction cache behavior for the hot path.
  \item \textbf{Profile-guided optimization}: use profiling data to determine which throw sites are hot and optimize them specifically.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{When to Use Error Codes Instead}\label{sec:error-codes-vs-exceptions}

The decision between exceptions and error codes depends on the expected frequency of errors:

\begin{definition}[Exception vs.\ Error Code Threshold]\label{def:eh-threshold}
Let $p$ be the probability of an error at a given call site, and $C_t$ be the cost of throwing an exception, $C_e$ be the cost of checking an error code. Exceptions are preferable when:
\[
  p \times C_t < C_e
\]
For zero-cost exceptions, $C_e \approx 0$ on the normal path, so exceptions are preferable when $p \times C_t$ is small---i.e., when errors are rare.
\end{definition}

