\section{Modern Exception Designs}\label{sec:modern-exception}

\subsection{Zig's Error Handling}\label{sec:zig-errors}

Zig uses a novel approach to error handling that combines the ergonomics of exceptions with the explicitness of error codes:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Zig error handling
  const FileError = error {
      NotFound,
      PermissionDenied,
      OutOfMemory,
  };
  
  fn readFile(name: []const u8) FileError![]u8 {
      const file = open(name) catch |err| {
          return err;  // Propagate error
      };
      defer close(file);
      return read(file);
  }
  
  // Usage:
  const contents = readFile("data.txt") catch |err| {
      switch (err) {
          .NotFound => return defaultData(),
          else => return err,
      }
  };
\end{verbatim}

Zig's approach has several advantages:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Errors are part of the type system: the return type \texttt{FileError![]u8} explicitly indicates that the function can fail.
  \item No hidden control flow: error propagation uses \texttt{catch} and \texttt{try}, which are explicit in the source.
  \item Zero overhead: errors are represented as error set values (small integers); no stack unwinding is needed.
  \item Exhaustive handling: the compiler ensures that all error cases are handled.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Swift's Typed Throws}\label{sec:swift-throws}

Swift 6.0 introduced typed throws, allowing functions to specify the exact error type they can throw:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Swift typed throws
  func parse(_ input: String) throws(ParseError) -> AST {
      // Can only throw ParseError
  }
  
  // Callers know the exact error type:
  do {
      let ast = try parse(source)
  } catch .syntaxError(let pos) {
      // Handle specific error
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Effect Handlers as the Future}\label{sec:effect-handlers-future}

Effect handlers (Section~\ref{sec:effect-handlers}) generalize exceptions by providing a composable mechanism for handling all kinds of computational effects. In the effect handler model:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Exceptions are just one kind of effect (the \emph{error} effect).
  \item Other effects include state, I/O, concurrency, and non-determinism.
  \item Handlers can be composed: a handler for one effect can be layered on top of a handler for another.
  \item Handlers can resume the computation, providing more flexibility than termination-only exceptions.
\end{itemize}

Languages like Eff, Koka, and Unikum explore effect handlers as a unified approach to exception handling and other computational effects.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:exception-summary}

Exception handling is a fundamental language feature that enables structured error handling and non-local control transfer. The zero-cost (table-based) implementation is the dominant approach in modern systems, providing no overhead on the normal path at the cost of expensive exception propagation. The compiler's role is to generate the exception tables, landing pads, and personality routines that enable the runtime to correctly unwind the stack and transfer control to the appropriate handler.

\begin{historical}
Exception handling has evolved from the ad hoc error codes of early systems to the sophisticated zero-cost mechanisms of modern languages. PL/I (1966) introduced one of the first exception handling mechanisms.CLU (1975) pioneered the termination model with handlers. C++ adopted exceptions in 1989, using the zero-cost table-based approach defined by the Itanium C++ ABI. Java (1995) added checked exceptions, which proved controversial and were not adopted by subsequent languages. The modern trend is toward minimal exception mechanisms (Rust's panics, Go's panic/recover) with explicit error types for recoverable errors, reflecting a growing understanding that exceptions should be reserved for truly exceptional situations.
\end{historical}
